  "Question": "Given the set of \\(n\\) numbers, \\(n\\gt1,\\) of which one is \\(1-\\dfrac1n\\) and all the others are \\(1.\\) The arithmetic mean of the \\(n\\) numbers is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(n-\\dfrac1n\\)",
    "C": "\\(n-\\dfrac1{n^2}\\)",
    "D": "\\(1-\\dfrac1{n^2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(1-\\dfrac1n-\\dfrac1{n^2}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "There are \\(n-1\\) entries equal to \\(1\\), together with the single entry \\(1-\\frac1n\\). Their sum is therefore\n\\[\n(n-1)+\\left(1-\\frac1n\\right)=n-\\frac1n.\n\\]\nThe arithmetic mean is the sum divided by the number of entries. Since \\(n\\) is an integer greater than \\(1\\), division by \\(n\\) is valid, and the mean is\n\\[\n\\frac{n-\\frac1n}{n}\n=1-\\frac1{n^2}.\n\\]\nThus the answer is \\(\\boxed{1-\\frac1{n^2}}\\), choice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
